#const AL 32
#const AX 33

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT
    land_percent 15
    base_size 6
  }

  AL
  {
    terrain_type SNOW
    land_percent 15
    base_size 6
  }

  AX
  {
    terrain_type DESERT
    land_percent 15
    base_size 6
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_61 - does a #const aliased to 32 really act as create_land?
Feeds: docs/known-issues.md BUG-005 piece 2, CREATION_PLAN A.2, parser-design
Sec.2.1 (verify #7)

WHY THIS IS ONE ID AND NOT A TABLE
BUG-005's largest input is 581 unknown-command warnings, every one of them the
same idiom in two maps. 24hr_Petra.rms:6 and 24hr_Holler.rms:248 both write

    #const L 32 /* Defining Land Creation Command */

and then use L as a land command, 384 and 197 times. Those 581 are the whole of
the corpus's aliasing population - a sweep of every #const in the corpus finds no
second word used as a command - so the fix needs ONE verified id, not the whole
equivalencies sheet the plan has been waiting on. The sheet is still worth
importing for the general case; it is not what this bug is blocked on.

WHAT IS ONLY INFERRED TODAY
That 32 is create_land at all. The single source is the map author's own comment
on that line. Sec.2.1's id-space model came from the community equivalencies
data and is supported by measurement in one place only - RMSTEST_56a/57 settled
that the comment-opening token is 69 - so the MODEL is measured and this
particular VALUE is not. Shipping a resolver on an author's comment would be
reference data sourced from a claim nobody checked.

METHOD
Three land commands, identical but for the terrain, in one file.

  create_land -> DIRT    the control that proves the script ran at all
  AL (32)     -> SNOW    the hypothesis
  AX (33)     -> DESERT  the orthogonal reading, expected to be boring

AX exists because "32 is create_land" and "any smallish number is create_land"
predict the same snow. It is deliberately LAST, so if an unknown command
swallows the block that follows it, the thing it swallows is the control arm
rather than the hypothesis.

READ-OFF, from probe_scenario.py's terrain histogram

  DIRT present, SNOW present, DESERT absent
      32 IS create_land. Write the one row, build the resolver, and BUG-005
      piece 2 closes at 581 warnings to zero.
  DIRT present, SNOW absent
      32 is not create_land, or a #const cannot reach command space at all. The
      author's comment is wrong and the whole tier-2 premise needs the sheet
      before anything is built. Note this reading also says those two maps do
      not generate the lands they appear to ask for.
  DIRT present, SNOW present, DESERT present
      The id is not what selects the command. Stop and rebuild the model; do not
      write a table on it.
  DIRT absent
      The run says nothing. Something ate the first command. Rerun before
      reading anything into the other two arms.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count, ONE RUN. The three arms are
independent and the reading is presence or absence, not proportion, so a second
seed adds nothing.

WHAT THIS STILL DOES NOT SETTLE
Only the one id. Every other alias in the equivalencies sheet stays unverified,
which is fine for BUG-005 and not fine for CREATION_PLAN A.2 - a general import
needs its own spot-checks, and this file is the pattern to copy for them.
*/
